    narrative: [
      'Muhammad ﷺ was born in Makkah around 570 CE, in the year the Arabs called Aam al-Fil — the Year of the Elephant. Abraha, the Abyssinian viceroy of Yemen, had built a grand church in Sana and resolved to divert the Arabs’ pilgrimage from the Kaaba; he marched on Makkah with war elephants. Abdul Muttalib, chief of Quraysh, told his people to withdraw to the hills and stood before Abraha asking only for his seized camels — “I am lord of the camels; the House has a Lord who will defend it.” Then came the miracle the Quran immortalises: flocks of birds (Tayran Ababil) pelted the army with baked-clay stones “and made them like eaten straw” (105:1–5). The year of that deliverance became the Arabs’ calendar mark — and in it, the deliverer’s Messenger was born.',
      'His lineage joins nobility of blood to nobility of purpose: Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abdul Muttalib (Shaybah) ibn Hashim ibn Abd Manaf — of Quraysh, descendants of Adnan, descendants of Ismail son of Ibrahim, the builder of the Kaaba. His mother Amina bint Wahb belonged to Banu Zuhrah, another respected Quraysh clan. Abdullah, his father, had died in Yathrib of illness while returning from a Syrian trade caravan, months before the birth — so the child opened his eyes an orphan. Reportedly Amina said a light shone from her with him that lit the palaces of Sham — a narration the scholars grade variously, but the symbolism stands: mercy to the worlds (21:107) had entered the world.',
      'Abdul Muttalib received the news with joy, carried the infant into the Kaaba, praised Allah and named him Muhammad — a rare name then, meaning “one who is repeatedly praised.” When asked why not an ancestral name, he answered: “I want Allah to praise him in heaven and people on earth.” The child was first suckled by Thuwaybah (Abu Lahab’s freed slave-girl, who had also nursed Hamza), then entrusted per Makkan custom to a Bedouin wet-nurse: Halima bint Abi Dhuayb of Banu Sad ibn Bakr, who took him though he was an orphan with little payment — and found her meagre fortunes blessed: her she-camel flowed with milk, her donkey outpaced the caravan, her flocks thrived while others starved.',
      'The desert years shaped him: pure Arabic of Banu Sad, hardy health, and the famous incident of the splitting of the chest (Shaqq as-Sadr) — two men in white opened his chest, removed a dark portion, washed the heart in a golden basin with Zamzam, and restored it — reported in Sahih Muslim from Anas. Frightened, Halima returned him to Amina at about age four or five. At six, Amina took him to Yathrib to visit Abdullah’s grave and her relatives of Banu Najjar; on the return journey she fell ill and died at Abwa, between Makkah and Yathrib. Umm Ayman (Barakah) brought the weeping orphan home — doubly orphaned, utterly alone except for Allah.',
      'Then the two guardians: first Abdul Muttalib, who seated the boy on his own mat by the Kaaba — honour no one else was allowed — and on his deathbed at the Prophet’s age of eight entrusted him to his son Abu Talib. Abu Talib, though burdened with children and little wealth, loved Muhammad ﷺ more than his own sons, slept him beside himself, and never left him behind. The pattern is unmistakable: every human support stripped away in turn — father, mother, grandfather — so that the one support remaining would be Allah alone, whose care the Quran later recalls: “Did He not find you an orphan and shelter you? He found you astray and guided you; He found you poor and enriched you” (93:6–8).',
    ],
