    lessons: [
      'Allah guards His chosen before revelation: sleep at the wedding parties, disgust at idols, honesty in shepherding — preparation precedes mission.',
      'Work dignifies: the future Prophet ﷺ earns his bread as a shepherd boy; no lawful work is shameful.',
      'The People of the Book knew the signs: Bahira’s testimony (and later Waraqa’s) reminds us the coming was foretold — enmity later was choice, not ignorance.',
    ],
    narrative: [
      'The Banu Sad years were the Prophet’s ﷺ university of the desert. Halima later narrated how her barren fortunes reversed the day she took the orphan: milk flowed, her old donkey raced ahead of the caravan, her sheep returned udder-full while neighbours’ starved — “take that blessed child’s flock’s path,” people said. The household lived in abundance until the day Muhammad ﷺ came running, pale, crying “two men in white split my belly!” — the Shaqq as-Sadr. Halima, fearing harm or jinn, returned him to Amina, confessing she feared for him. Amina, serene, answered: “My son will be great.” Modern readers may debate the metaphysics; the early community preserved it as the divine surgery that purified the vessel of revelation — Sahih Muslim records it from Anas ibn Malik.',
      'Then the hammer-blows of orphanhood. Amina’s death at Abwa left a six-year-old who had just wept at his father’s grave now weeping at his mother’s. Umm Ayman carried him to Makkah, where eighty-year-old Abdul Muttalib enfolded him — seating him on the chief’s mat at the Kaaba’s shade, stroking his back, saying “my son will be great.” Two years later the old chief too died, gathering his sons: who will take Muhammad? Az-Zubayr declined in favour of Abu Talib — full brother of Abdullah — who though poorest took the boy, declaring to his family that Muhammad ﷺ would eat with them from one bowl. The Quran’s later question rings over these graves: “Did He not find you an orphan and shelter you?” (93:6) — shelter arriving precisely as each shelter fell.',
      'Around age twelve came the Syrian journey. Abu Talib, joining a Quraysh caravan, at first refused the boy’s tearful clinging — then relented. At Busra the caravan camped near the cell of Bahira, a Christian monk versed in the old scriptures, who had never before received them but now prepared a feast: he had watched from his window a cloud shading one traveller, and a tree lowering its branches over him when he sat. Examining the boy — the description in his books, the Seal of Prophethood (Khatam an-Nubuwwah, a raised fleshy mark between the shoulders) — Bahira seized Abu Talib’s hand: “This is the master of the worlds; Allah will send him as mercy. Take him back and guard him from the Jews — by Allah, if they see what I have seen they will harm him.” Abu Talib cut his trading short and returned. The episode, preserved by al-Tirmidhi and Ibn Hisham, is the first external testimony to the coming mission.',
      'Boyhood passed into labour. The Prophet ﷺ later said: “No prophet was sent but he herded sheep” — and when asked if he had, replied he herded Makkans’ sheep at Qararit for a few coins (Sahih al-Bukhari). Shepherding in the Hijazi hills taught what no palace could: patience with straying flocks, courage against wolves, tenderness to the weak, long solitary hours under a sky that preaches One Creator. He also joined Quraysh in the sacrilegious war (Harb al-Fijar) as a youth, gathering arrows for his uncles — witnessing how tribal honour bled for nothing.',
      'Most telling is what did not happen. Twice, he later narrated, he resolved to attend Makkan night-merriment — music, wine, dancing girls at a wedding — and twice Allah cast sleep upon him till the sun’s heat woke him; “I never again intended evil” (Ibn Hisham). He never touched wine, never bowed to Hubal, Lat or Uzza, never ate meat slaughtered to idols (he rebuked even Zayd ibn Amr’s offered idol-meat before revelation). While Makkan youths sank into the vices revelation would later forbid, the orphan shepherd was being kept — visibly, memorably — until the day the trust of revelation could descend on a vessel already clean.',
    ],
    references: 'Quran 93:6; Sahih al-Bukhari (shepherding for Qararit); Sahih Muslim (Shaqq as-Sadr); Sunan al-Tirmidhi and Ibn Hisham (Bahira at Busra)',
